{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I'm Dr. Lee, one of your hospitalists. You're here today for your fifth cycle of high-dose methotrexate for your CNS lymphoma. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I'm okay overall. A bit tired, but no major issues. I’ve been having this dry eye on the right side lately, and a sore spot on my lower lip."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for mentioning that. The lip lesion could be related to herpes, especially since you’ve had it before. That’s why you’re on acyclovir. We’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. I thought it might be from the chemo. And the dry eye—is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be irritation or dryness from other factors. We’ll check with ophthalmology if it persists. For now, artificial tears might help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And my foot pain—it’s still there, mostly in the right foot. It flares up when I walk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we see that. It’s thought to be neuropathic, possibly from the biopsy. You’re on gabapentin 400 mg three times a day, which is a good dose. You mentioned wanting to try tramadol instead of oxycodone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’m a bit worried about oxycodone. I don’t want to get dependent. Is tramadol safer?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Tramadol is another option, but we usually switch later if needed. For now, we’ll keep oxycodone available for breakthrough pain and monitor how you do."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. I just want to manage it without getting worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. We’ll keep supporting you. Now, your labs this morning look stable. Your liver enzymes are slightly elevated, but not as bad as last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So that’s the transaminitis again? Is that from the chemo?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, methotrexate can affect the liver. But yours is mild, and we’re giving extra leucovorin and bicarbonate to protect you and help clear the drug."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the dexamethasone—I’ve been taking it daily, but I know I was supposed to taper. Should I stop now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll taper it slowly after discharge. You’ll take 1 mg daily for four days, then half a tablet for four days, then stop. Stopping suddenly can cause adrenal issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t know that. Thanks for explaining."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll also keep you on ranitidine for stomach protection since you’re on steroids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my asthma? I’ve been using my inhaler as usual."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your asthma is well-controlled. Keep using your fluticasone-salmeterol and montelukast. Only use albuterol if you feel tightness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I don’t want to end up short of breath again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor closely. Now, you’ll get the methotrexate infusion around noon. We’ll give sodium bicarbonate first to keep your urine alkaline and help the drug clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I need to drink a lot, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ll also check your urine pH frequently to make sure it stays above 7.5."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And when do I start the leucovorin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "About 24 hours after the infusion starts. You’ll take it every six hours to protect your cells from methotrexate side effects."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I’ll be here for a few days?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2175-06-23 11:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, usually three to four days. We’ll check your methotrexate levels daily until they drop low enough."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. You’re doing well after the infusion. The dose was 8 grams per square meter. We’re keeping you on IV fluids with bicarbonate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Any side effects so far? I feel a little nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common. We’ll give you ondansetron for nausea. Let us know if it gets worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. And my headache this morning—it’s better now, but I get them often."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We can give oxycodone for headache if needed. But they’ve been ongoing, so we don’t think it’s from today’s treatment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I just worry it’s the lymphoma getting worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Actually, your MRI from yesterday shows improvement. The tumor in your right frontal lobe has shrunk significantly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That’s great to hear!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, excellent response. No new lesions. The swelling around the tumor has also decreased."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s such a relief. I was so scared it wasn’t working."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2175-06-23 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s definitely working. You’ll continue every two weeks for several more cycles."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your 24-hour methotrexate level came back low—excellent clearance. That means your kidneys are processing the drug well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Very good. It reduces your risk of kidney or liver problems. We’ll keep checking levels until they’re undetectable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I can go home soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly tomorrow, if your levels stay low and you’re feeling well. You’ll take leucovorin and bicarbonate at home for one more day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I need to take both after I leave?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, every six hours for leucovorin and bicarbonate. It ensures the methotrexate is fully cleared from your body."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And my enoxaparin—I keep that up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, twice daily. You’re on it for the DVT and PE you had during your first cycle."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2175-06-24 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Right. I almost forgot about that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi. Your 48-hour methotrexate level is now undetectable. That’s perfect. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take leucovorin and bicarbonate every six hours for one full day. Continue your other meds: gabapentin, oxycodone as needed, and dexamethasone taper."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And when’s my next chemo?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "In two weeks. Your neuro-oncologist’s office will confirm the appointment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if my lip sore gets worse?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep using acyclovir. If it ulcerates or spreads, call your doctor. Same if you develop fever or shortness of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my foot pain—I’ll keep the gabapentin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2175-06-25 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We can discuss tramadol at your next visit if you’d like."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2175-06-26 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re cleared to go home. You did very well with this cycle—no complications, excellent drug clearance, and your MRI shows response."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2175-06-26 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel relieved. I’ll take the pills as directed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2175-06-26 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. Call if you have nausea, rash, mouth sores, or worsening pain. Otherwise, see you in two weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2175-06-26 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for taking such good care of me."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2175-06-23 10:42:00",
    "admission_end": "2175-06-26 11:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old woman with primary central nervous system lymphoma, ulcerative colitis, asthma, and a history of DVT/PE was admitted for her fifth cycle of high-dose methotrexate chemotherapy. She tolerated the treatment well with no acute complications. Her 48-hour methotrexate level showed excellent clearance. An MRI of the head demonstrated a marked reduction in the size of the right frontal lobe lesion, indicating a positive response to therapy. Chronic right foot pain, likely neuropathic, persisted and was managed with gabapentin and oxycodone. Dexamethasone was continued during admission and a tapering plan was provided at discharge. She developed a new lip lesion, presumed herpes-related, and was maintained on acyclovir prophylaxis. She remained stable throughout the admission, with mild transaminitis and anemia monitored but not requiring intervention. She was discharged home with instructions to complete a final day of leucovorin and bicarbonate, and to follow up in two weeks for the next chemotherapy cycle.",
    "problems": [
      "Encounter for antineoplastic chemotherapy",
      "Primary central nervous system lymphoma, unspecified site, extranodal and solid organ sites",
      "Ulcerative colitis, unspecified",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Personal history of pulmonary embolism",
      "Multiple sclerosis",
      "Pain in limb",
      "Nausea alone",
      "Anemia in neoplastic disease",
      "Nonspecific elevation of levels of transaminase or lactic acid dehydrogenase [LDH]",
      "Antineoplastic and immunosuppressive drugs causing adverse effects in therapeutic use",
      "Headache",
      "Mononeuritis of lower limb, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
